tis the season for resolutions you may  have given up yours already where are we  were january fifteenth it may be too  late to even speak to them but here we  are there's an icon in the upper right  corner of my imac screen that says  resolutions 2012 haven't even looked at  it for a week a little scared of it and  i'm not sure that's where the focus for  this weekend should be this season of  Epiphany this mark season of Martin  Luther King Day I think we are called to  a different kind of role than a self  improvement plan that inward-looking  guilt-inducing sense that I should be a  superhuman super moral super perfect  kind of person I think we're called to a  different kind of faith and I think  that's what our scriptures speak to  today st. John wrote the word became  flesh and dwelt among us full of grace  and truth that's the scripture we read  at Christmas every year but we're past  Christmas were into a new season and I  think sometimes the Word of God needs a  helper think sometimes the Word of God  needs a midwife or a messenger or a  middleman and I think that is our role  our scriptures today are about unlikely  unrecognized heroes those who at just  the right moment were the connection  between the Word of God and those who  are looking for God the middle Midwife  call it the middle man called the  messenger they usually aren't the heroes  of the story that's reserved for the  mother or the child for the buyer or the  seller for the sender or receiver the  Word of God the call of God goes out but  often it takes a midwife or a messenger  to put the call through in this church  in the season of Epiphany we celebrate  God's Word going out to the world and  for this season the messengers are  Rose our passage from First Samuel  begins the word of the Lord was rare in  those days visions were not widespread  sounds like today perhaps but we are in  11th century BC in Israel we are at the  sanctuary in Shiloh long before the  Jerusalem Temple and Eli the 